Title:
SSHDESK — Interactive Remote Desktop Over a Single SSH Session (Terminal-Rendered)

Type:
GitHub Tool

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
- SSHDESK provides a fully interactive remote desktop streamed entirely through an existing SSH session and rendered inside the client terminal.  
- It solves “remote GUI access without extra services” by avoiding VNC/RDP/web servers, additional ports, or separate authentication—OpenSSH handles auth and a forced command launches the desktop.  
- The project implements screen capture, delta/tile-based updates, and keyboard/mouse/resize event transport over the SSH PTY, with adaptive rendering (Kitty graphics protocol for sharp PNG tiles; ANSI/Unicode/ASCII fallbacks for generic terminals).  
- It supports multiple host backends: X11 and common Wayland environments (GNOME via Mutter RemoteDesktop + PipeWire/GStreamer; others via tools like grim/spectacle and ydotool), plus macOS (Quartz capture/input) and experimental Windows hosting (SendInput; Session 0 caveats with OpenSSH service).  
- It also includes an “agent” command interface (screenshot, click, type, etc.) and helper client (`sshdesk-remote`) for structured JSON automation over SSH.  
- Useful for red teams/pentesters needing low-friction interactive access on locked-down networks, and for defenders/admins evaluating SSH forced-command exposure and console-equivalent risk.

Technical Focus:
- OpenSSH forced-command design and PTY-based multiplexing (desktop + control)
- Terminal rendering pipelines (Kitty graphics protocol, ANSI/truecolor fallbacks)
- Screen capture + diffing (tile/cell updates, static-frame suppression, FPS/latency control)
- Cross-platform input injection (XTest, Mutter RemoteDesktop API, ydotool/uinput, Quartz, SendInput)
- Wayland capture/input strategies (PipeWire/GStreamer, compositor-specific tooling)
- Automation interface over SSH (bounded commands, NDJSON/JSON session mode)

Use Cases:
- Remote interactive GUI access over SSH when only port 22 is allowed
- Post-exploitation “desktop-in-terminal” control without deploying VNC/RDP listeners
- Operator/agent automation: scripted screenshots, clicks, typing, and session workflows
- Testing terminal graphics compatibility and bandwidth/latency tradeoffs for remote UI streaming
- Defensive assessment of forced-command SSH accounts and “console access” risk modeling

Keywords:
SSHDESK, OpenSSH, forced command, PTY, SSH, remote desktop, terminal rendering, Kitty graphics protocol, ANSI, truecolor, PNG tiles, X11, Wayland, GNOME Mutter RemoteDesktop, PipeWire, GStreamer, XTest, ydotool, /dev/uinput, Quartz, SendInput, tmux, Tailscale